4. Operational Processing Algorithm
Stage 1 – Arrival
The tractor unit enters the logistics hub.

The system automatically creates a transport session.

Collected information:

Arrival timestamp
Tractor ID
Driver ID (if required)
Semi-trailer ID
Transport order reference
Stage 2 – Trailer Detachment
The driver disconnects the semi-trailer.

The tractor immediately leaves the hub and becomes available for another transport operation.

Only the semi-trailer continues through the automated system.

Stage 3 – Automatic Identification
The trailer passes through the identification portal.

Subsystems:

RFID reader
OCR cameras
License plate recognition
Trailer number recognition
Output:

Trailer Identity Verified
or

Identity Mismatch
Stage 4 – Weight Verification
The semi-trailer passes over the Weigh-In-Motion (WIM) system.

Measured parameters:

Gross weight
Axle loads
Weight distribution
Decision:

PASS / FAIL

Stage 5 – Dimension Verification
Laser scanners create a complete 3D profile.

Measured parameters:

Height
Width
Length
Load contour
Decision:

PASS / FAIL

Stage 6 – Security Inspection
Depending on the corridor configuration, the trailer passes through:

X-ray scanner
Radiation monitor
Thermal cameras
Hazard detection sensors
Output:

Security Status

Stage 7 – Rear Inspection & Seal Verification
This stage ensures cargo integrity before the trailer enters the international corridor.

Rear-mounted cameras perform:

OCR of seal number
Detection of seal presence
AI verification of seal integrity
Comparison with customs declaration
Rear image recording for audit purposes
Decision criteria:

Seal present
Seal number matches declaration
Seal physically intact
No evidence of tampering
Output:

Seal Verified
or

Seal Verification Failed
Stage 8 – Data Fusion
All inspection results are consolidated into a single digital transport record.

Inputs include:

RFID
OCR
WIM
3D dimensions
Security scan
Seal verification
Customs declaration
Risk assessment
This record becomes the authoritative digital representation of the freight unit throughout its journey.

9. Decision Engine
The Decision Engine evaluates all available information.

Possible outcomes are:

PASS
All validation checks completed successfully.

Result:

Assign platform.

SECONDARY INSPECTION
One or more checks require human verification.

Examples:

unreadable RFID;
damaged seal;
weight discrepancy;
OCR mismatch;
incomplete documentation;
abnormal scanner results.
Result:

Automatically divert the trailer to the Inspection Track.

HOLD
Temporary operational hold.

Examples:

communication failure;
customs system unavailable;
platform unavailable.
The trailer remains within the logistics hub until processing can continue.

REJECT
Transport authorization denied.

The trailer is removed from the automated transport process and handled according to customs and border authority procedures.

10. Inspection Track Workflow
Trailers requiring additional inspection are automatically routed to a dedicated rail siding.

Inspection activities may include:

manual customs inspection;
manual seal verification;
cargo inspection;
document verification;
re-weighing;
secondary scanning.
After successful resolution, the trailer may return to the main processing workflow.

11. Platform Assignment
For approved trailers, the system:

assigns the next available autonomous rail platform;
verifies platform availability;
secures the trailer to the platform;
confirms locking status;
authorizes departure.
12. Autonomous Corridor Transport
During transport, the platform remains continuously powered through an electrified contact rail system supplied by traction substations.

The electrical infrastructure is supported by:

photovoltaic generation (PV);
biogas-powered generation;
Battery Energy Storage System (BESS);
utility grid connection (when required).
The platforms themselves do not require onboard charging, reducing vehicle weight and simplifying fleet operation.

13. Continuous Border Inspection
Instead of stopping at the physical border, customs and border-control procedures continue while the platform is moving.

The digital transport record accompanies the trailer throughout the journey.

Additional telemetry may include:

platform position;
speed;
vibration monitoring;
cargo integrity;
seal status;
power consumption.
14. Arrival at Destination Hub
Upon arrival:

platform identification;
unloading;
final seal verification (if required);
customs release;
trailer handover.
The trailer is connected to a domestic tractor and leaves the logistics hub.

